            "comparisons": [
                {
                    "title": "Biological computing vs traditional AI hardware",
                    "left": {
                        "title": "CL1 neuron rack",
                        "points": [
                            "~30 W per unit; full rack 850–1,000 W",
                            "Neurons adapt and learn from electrical feedback",
                            "Cells stay viable up to 6 months"
                        ]
                    },
                    "right": {
                        "title": "GPU-based AI servers",
                        "points": [
                            "Single AI GPU can draw ~700 W or more",
                            "Fixed silicon; learning done in software",
                            "Hardware runs for years but at high energy cost"
                        ]
                    }
                }
            ],
            "keyPoints": [
                "Neurons are grown from human induced pluripotent stem cells (from donor blood or skin) onto a multielectrode silicon chip",
                "In 2022 the same team's DishBrain showed 800,000 neurons learning Pong in about 5 minutes",
                "The Singapore rack at NUS Medicine is the world's first independently operated biologically integrated server rack",
                "Sold at $35,000 per unit, ~$20,000/unit in 30-unit racks, or remote 'Wetware-as-a-Service' access for ~$300/week",
                "Target uses: drug discovery, disease modeling and energy-efficient adaptive computing — still a research prototype"
            ],
